meta {
  name: Update Household Policy
  type: http
  seq: 13
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/households/{{policyEditHouseholdId}}/policies/{{policyEditPolicyId}}
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "premium": {{policyEditPremium}}
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns the PolicySummary the household card renders", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("id");
    expect(body).to.have.property("policyNumber");
    expect(body).to.have.property("policyType");
    expect(body).to.have.property("carrier");
    expect(body).to.have.property("active");
    expect(body).to.have.property("policyStatus");
    expect(body).to.have.property("premium");
    expect(body).to.have.property("items");
    expect(body).to.have.property("effectiveDate");
    expect(body).to.have.property("expirationDate");
  });

  test("carries the renewal anchor — the reason this route exists", function () {
    // `UpdatePolicyResult` (what `PATCH /policies/:id` returns) has no field for
    // this. The household card leads with it, so losing it here would defeat the
    // ticket.
    expect(res.getBody()).to.have.property("renewalDate");
  });

  test("the premium is written back unchanged", function () {
    const sent = bru.getVar("policyEditPremium");
    if (sent !== undefined && sent !== null) {
      expect(res.getBody().premium).to.equal(sent);
    }
  });

  test("never echoes internals", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("agencyId");
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("branchId");
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("isTestRecord");
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("legacySmartSuiteId");
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /households/:id/policies/:policyId

  Correct a policy **from the household page** (PAC-126).

  - **Module:** `clients` OR `crm_service`
  - **Permission:** `clients:write` **OR** `crm_service:write`
  - **Data scope:** `own` collapses to **branch**

  ## Why this exists beside `PATCH /policies/:id`

  That endpoint is the Lead Detail **Sold card's** quick edit. It gates on
  `deal_audits:write` and resolves `own` scope through the policy's **deal**,
  which is right for a producer correcting their own sale and wrong for every
  policy this route is about. Two consequences, both fatal here:

  - a **CSR holds no `deal_audits` permission at all**, so the service team gets
    a 403 on the endpoint they need most;
  - `own` scope reads ownership off `deal.producerId`, and a **migrated policy
    has no deal**, so it reports as 404 — "masking something unattributable is
    the safe direction", which it is, for a sales record.

  A household policy is neither unattributable nor a sales record: it belongs to
  the household in the path. So the household supplies both the permission and
  the scope, and the policy is re-checked against it — a policy that is not this
  household's is a **404**, never a fall-through to editing someone else's record.

  Only the *finding* differs. The mutation is `PoliciesService.applyUpdate`, one
  copy shared with the Sold card, so the renewal re-derivation, the item-count
  normalization, the `policyNumberKey` upkeep and the edit log cannot drift
  between the two routes.

  ⚠ Producers also hold `clients:write` (PAC-38 gave them contact editing) and
  therefore reach this. Deliberate: every other write on this controller — add
  member, name the primary, end a membership — is already open to them under the
  same branch-collapsed scope.

  ## Body
  Identical to `PATCH /policies/:id` — see **Sold Deals › Update Policy** for the
  full field table, the `null`-clears-vs-omitted rule, the `items` normalization
  and the `status` vocabulary. Every field optional, object must not be empty.

  ## `renewalDate` is derived, never sent
  `policies.renewalDate` is the anchor every renewal call counts backwards from,
  and the schema is explicit: *"never accept it from a client — an anchor that
  disagrees with the effective date schedules real calls to real clients on the
  wrong day."* It is **not** a body field.

  Send **`effectiveDate`** instead: the server re-derives the anchor from it and
  the policy's term (6 months for the auto family, 12 otherwise) and returns the
  new value. That is the whole mechanism behind this ticket — the operator types
  the date on the declaration page, and the date they cannot compute appears on
  the card. Changing `policyType` re-derives it too. Clearing `effectiveDate`
  clears the anchor, because there is then nothing to count from.

  ## Response
  A **`PolicySummary`** — the shape the household page already renders, so the
  card swaps the row in place rather than refetching the whole 360° assembly for
  a one-field correction. Note this is *not* `PATCH /policies/:id`'s
  `UpdatePolicyResult`, which has no `renewalDate` field at all.

  ## Edit log
  Writes the same `activities` change row as the Sold card's edit (PAC-65 #9),
  covering policy number, type, carrier, premium, items, effective date,
  expiration date and status. Readable by `agency:changelogs:read` — Agency Owner
  and Branch Manager only, so a CSR's corrections are recorded but not readable
  by them.

  ## Errors
  | Status | When |
  |---|---|
  | 400 | Empty object, an unknown `status`, or a malformed date |
  | 401 | Missing/expired bearer token |
  | 403 | Both `clients` and `crm_service` disabled, or the caller holds neither write |
  | 404 | Policy or household outside the caller's scope, or the policy is not this household's |
}
